<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
    The published documentation. What is rendered is not `documentation/` itself
    but the copy `bin/cli documentation:prepare` writes, because only that copy
    has the links a reader without the checkout can follow. On one machine that
    step and this renderer are one command:

        bin/cli documentation:preview

    An error fails the build. What used to stand here was the one warning this
    repository would not remove — `typo3_icon_lookup` describes itself with the
    Fluid tag `<core:icon>`, which CommonMark read as an autolink and the
    renderer then could not resolve. reStructuredText reads no such thing, so
    the tool's `description` renders as written and the exception is gone with
    the parser that needed it — `D-DOC-029`.
-->
<guides
    xmlns="https://www.phpdoc.org/guides"
    input=".site/source"
    output=".site/html"
    input-format="rst"
    theme="soul"
    fail-on-error="true"
    links-are-relative="true"
>
    <!--
        The last line of the footer. `copyright` is the renderer's own project
        setting rather than the theme's, so it stands here beside the title and
        the theme prints whatever it is handed; the licence it names is the one
        `composer.json` declares.
    -->
    <project title="TYPO3 Dev Companion" copyright="© 2026 The TYPO3 Project — MIT licensed"/>
    <!--
        The rail and the trail are the `toctree` each `index.rst` carries, which
        is what the corpus writes now that it can. `automatic-menu` stood here
        instead and derived the same thing from the directories, because the
        Markdown parser had no directive to say it with and marked every page an
        orphan without it. An explicit tree says the order as well, which a
        directory listing cannot.
    -->
    <!--
        Load-bearing, and `input-format` above does not imply it: the render
        dies in the theme's own `body/code.html.twig`, which colours a code
        block with a `highlight` filter only this extension registers. The
        Markdown parser was declared beside it and is gone with the format —
        the reStructuredText one is registered by the CLI itself.
    -->
    <extension class="phpDocumentor\Guides\Code\DependencyInjection\CodeExtension"/>
    <!--
        The theme `theme="soul"` selects: this element is what makes it exist,
        and everything the bar, the tab and the footer say that is not the
        project title is set here rather than in a template copied into this
        checkout.
    -->
    <extension class="TYPO3\Soul\GuidesTheme\DependencyInjection\SoulExtension">
        <signet>images/signet-m.svg</signet>
        <!--
            The mark is redrawn per optical size rather than scaled, and a
            browser picks between the files by the slot it needs — which a
            media query inside one file cannot do, since it only ever sees its
            own viewport.
        -->
        <favicon href="images/signet-s.svg" sizes="16x16"/>
        <favicon href="images/signet-l.svg" sizes="32x32"/>
        <brand>TYPO3</brand>
        <product>Dev Companion</product>
        <cdn>jsdelivr</cdn>
        <!--
            The four sections, and only those. The bar is where a reader goes
            from anywhere; the rail below it is the section they are in, and a
            manual with every page in the bar has said nothing.

            No `label` on any of them: a section is called what its own
            `:navigation-title:` says, so the name lives on the page the link
            leads to and the two cannot drift apart. The attribute is for a link
            whose target is not in this tree, which none of these is.
        -->
        <navigation>
            <link href="/usage/index"/>
            <link href="/server/index"/>
            <link href="/contributing/index"/>
            <link href="/records/index"/>
        </navigation>
        <footer>
            <social href="https://github.com/TYPO3/dev-companion" label="GitHub"/>
            <!--
                What this is, under the mark and on every page. The front page
                says it at length in the hero, which a reader who arrived on a
                tool page has not seen — so what stands here is the whole claim
                in two sentences: what it answers, and what it may touch.
            -->
            <note>A local MCP server that answers a coding agent's TYPO3 questions, bound to the versions each answer holds for. The client starts it beside the project, where it reads the installation and writes nothing into it.</note>
        </footer>
    </extension>
</guides>
